    "['isis enable [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, enabling IS-IS on an interface is done under `protocols isis interface` hierarchy\\n2. Juniper uses ge-x/y/z.0 naming for GigabitEthernet interfaces (with .0 for unit 0) and lo0.0 for loopback\\n3. The IS-IS instance number (1 in Huawei) isn't explicitly needed in Juniper's basic configuration as it typically supports a single IS-IS instance by default\\n4. Each interface requires a separate configuration statement\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, enabling IS-IS on an interface is done under `protocols isis interface` hierarchy\\n2. Juniper uses ge-x/y/z.0 naming for GigabitEthernet interfaces (with .0 for unit 0) and lo0.0 for loopback\\n3. The IS-IS instance number (1 in Huawei) isn't explicitly needed in Juniper's basic configuration as it typically supports a single IS-IS instance by default\\n4. Each interface requires a separate configuration statement\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, enabling IS-IS on an interface is done under `protocols isis interface` hierarchy\\n2. Juniper uses ge-x/y/z.0 naming for GigabitEthernet interfaces (with .0 for unit 0) and lo0.0 for loopback\\n3. The IS-IS instance number (1 in Huawei) isn't explicitly needed in Juniper's basic configuration as it typically supports a single IS-IS instance by default\\n4. Each interface requires a separate configuration statement\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0'}]]": 1,
    "['isis enable [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface <interface-name>', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Huawei command `isis enable 1` for enabling IS-IS on an interface:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface <interface-name>\\n##\\n\\nNote:\\n1. Replace `<interface-name>` with the actual interface name (e.g., `ge-0/0/0`, `lo0`, etc.)\\n2. In Juniper, you would typically configure IS-IS globally first with `set protocols isis`, then enable it on specific interfaces\\n3. The Huawei command enables IS-IS process 1 on the interface, while Juniper doesn't require specifying the process ID in this context (unless you're running multiple IS-IS instances)\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface <interface-name>'}]]": 1,
    "['isis enable [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, enabling IS-IS on an interface is done under `protocols isis interface` hierarchy.\\n- The `.0` suffix is added to interface names to specify the logical unit (default in Juniper).\\n- Loopback interface is typically named `lo0` in Juniper.\\n- The IS-IS instance number (`1`) isn't needed in Juniper's basic configuration as it supports only one IS-IS instance by default. If multiple instances were needed, you would use routing-instances.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, enabling IS-IS on an interface is done under `protocols isis interface` hierarchy.\\n- The `.0` suffix is added to interface names to specify the logical unit (default in Juniper).\\n- Loopback interface is typically named `lo0` in Juniper.\\n- The IS-IS instance number (`1`) isn't needed in Juniper's basic configuration as it supports only one IS-IS instance by default. If multiple instances were needed, you would use routing-instances.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0.0\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, enabling IS-IS on an interface is done under `protocols isis interface` hierarchy.\\n- The `.0` suffix is added to interface names to specify the logical unit (default in Juniper).\\n- Loopback interface is typically named `lo0` in Juniper.\\n- The IS-IS instance number (`1`) isn't needed in Juniper's basic configuration as it supports only one IS-IS instance by default. If multiple instances were needed, you would use routing-instances.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0'}]]": 1,
    "['isis prefix-sid absolute [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0 level 2 prefix-sid absolute 153710', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0 level 2 prefix-sid absolute 153710\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if using Juniper's newer unified configuration style (where `lo0.0` is the loopback interface name):\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0 source-packet-routing prefix-sid index 153710\\n##\\n\\nNote: Juniper's exact implementation may vary based on the Junos OS version. The first version is classic ISIS configuration, while the second reflects Juniper's newer Segment Routing (source-packet-routing) syntax. Both achieve the same result as Huawei's command - assigning an absolute prefix SID to the loopback interface in IS-IS.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0 level 2 prefix-sid absolute 153710'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0 source-packet-routing prefix-sid index 153710', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0 level 2 prefix-sid absolute 153710\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if using Juniper's newer unified configuration style (where `lo0.0` is the loopback interface name):\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0 source-packet-routing prefix-sid index 153710\\n##\\n\\nNote: Juniper's exact implementation may vary based on the Junos OS version. The first version is classic ISIS configuration, while the second reflects Juniper's newer Segment Routing (source-packet-routing) syntax. Both achieve the same result as Huawei's command - assigning an absolute prefix SID to the loopback interface in IS-IS.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0 source-packet-routing prefix-sid index 153710'}]]":